(function () {
  const SUB = window.SUBJECTS && window.SUBJECTS.finance;
  if (!SUB) return;
  const pages = [{"id": "u1-new", "title": "Unit I-B · Complete notes - finance functions, objectives, TVM, annuity, bonds", "html": "<div class='box'><b>UNIT I - Introduction to Finance (10 lectures).</b> Complete notes with formulas and worked examples, written for the new syllabus (Syllabus image FINANCE/syllabus.jpeg) from your Complete Notes file, unit slides and Unit 1-3 notes.</div><h2>1.1 Meaning and functions of finance</h2><p><b>Finance</b> is the art and science of managing money. It is often called the <b>lifeblood</b> of a business because every business decision has a financial side. The <b>finance function</b> is the set of managerial activities concerned with <b>procuring (raising) and effectively utilising funds</b> for the business.</p><h4>The three core decisions</h4><ol><li><b>Investment decision</b> - where to invest the firm's funds (which assets and projects).</li><li><b>Financing decision</b> - how to raise the funds (the mix of debt and equity).</li><li><b>Dividend decision</b> - how much profit to distribute and how much to retain.</li></ol><h4>Supporting functions</h4><ul><li><b>Financial planning and forecasting</b> - estimating future fund requirements.</li><li><b>Financial control</b> - checking that funds are used efficiently through budgets and variance analysis.</li><li><b>Liquidity management</b> - always having enough cash for short-term obligations.</li><li><b>Profit planning</b> - cost-volume-profit analysis to guide pricing and output.</li></ul><h4>Need and importance of the finance function</h4><ul><li>Funds are needed to start, run and expand a business.</li><li>Sound finance keeps the firm solvent and liquid.</li><li>It links all departments through budgets.</li><li>It helps to reduce the cost of funds and to maximise value.</li></ul><h2>1.2 Objectives of financial management: profit maximisation vs wealth maximisation</h2><h4>Profit maximisation (traditional approach)</h4><p>The firm should aim to earn the highest possible profit. Profit measures efficiency and is needed for survival and growth.</p><b>Limitations</b><ul><li><b>Ignores the time value of money</b> - a rupee of profit today and after five years are treated alike.</li><li><b>Ignores risk</b> - a firm may chase profit through very risky decisions.</li><li><b>Ambiguous</b> - profit may mean short-term or long-term, before or after tax, total or per share.</li><li><b>Ignores quality and timing of earnings</b> - whether profits are regular and sustainable.</li><li>Can lead to short-term thinking and neglect of stakeholders.</li></ul><h4>Shareholder wealth maximisation (modern approach)</h4><p>The goal is to maximise the <b>market value of shareholders' wealth</b>, shown by the market price of the equity share. <b>Wealth = number of shares x market price per share.</b></p><ul><li>Considers the <b>time value of money</b> (cash flows are discounted).</li><li>Considers <b>risk</b> (risky cash flows are discounted at a higher rate).</li><li><b>Precise</b> - market price is an observable figure.</li><li><b>Long-term</b> - share price reflects the market's view of future earning power.</li></ul><table><tr><th>Basis</th><th>Profit maximisation</th><th>Wealth maximisation</th></tr><tr><td>Focus</td><td>Short-term accounting profit</td><td>Long-term market value of shares</td></tr><tr><td>Time value of money</td><td>Ignored</td><td>Considered</td></tr><tr><td>Risk</td><td>Ignored</td><td>Considered</td></tr><tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Ambiguous (many profit measures)</td><td>Precise (market price)</td></tr><tr><td>Time horizon</td><td>Short term</td><td>Long term</td></tr><tr><td>Basis of measure</td><td>Accounting profit</td><td>Cash flows</td></tr></table><p><b>Stakeholders:</b> a modern view also asks the firm to care for employees, customers, lenders and society, because long-term wealth depends on their support.</p><h2>1.3 Various decisions in financial management</h2><table><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Key considerations</th></tr><tr><td>Investment decision (capital budgeting)</td><td>How the firm's funds are invested in assets to earn the highest return for a given risk. Long-term (capital budgeting - Unit II) and short-term (working capital - Unit IV)</td><td>Expected return, risk, cost of capital; usually the most important decision because profitability depends on it</td></tr><tr><td>Financing decision (capital structure)</td><td>How funds are raised - the mix of debt and equity (Unit III)</td><td>Cost of each source, risk (debt raises financial risk), control (equity dilutes control)</td></tr><tr><td>Dividend decision</td><td>How much profit is paid as dividend and how much is retained (Unit IV)</td><td>Retained earnings are cheap internal finance; shareholders also want regular income</td></tr></table><p>The three decisions are <b>interlinked</b>: dividends reduce retained earnings, which increases the need for outside finance; investment needs decide how much finance is required.</p><h2>1.4 Time value of money (TVM)</h2><p>A rupee received <b>today is worth more than a rupee received in the future</b>, because today's rupee can be invested to earn a return.</p><h4>Reasons for the time value of money</h4><ul><li><b>Opportunity to earn</b> - money in hand can be invested to earn interest.</li><li><b>Inflation</b> - purchasing power falls over time.</li><li><b>Risk and uncertainty</b> - future receipts are less certain.</li><li><b>Preference for present consumption.</b></li></ul><h4>Future value (compounding)</h4><p><b>Future value (FV)</b> is the value of a present sum at a future date when it earns interest, with interest itself earning interest (compounding).</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> FV = PV x (1 + r)<sup>n</sup> &nbsp;&nbsp; where r = rate of interest per period, n = number of periods; (1 + r)<sup>n</sup> is the future value interest factor</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - future value</b><br>Rs 10,000 is invested at 8% compound interest for 5 years.<br>FV = 10,000 x (1.08)<sup>5</sup> = 10,000 x 1.4693 = <b>Rs 14,693.28</b>.</div><h4>Present value (discounting)</h4><p><b>Present value (PV)</b> is the current worth of a sum to be received in future, discounted at the required rate of return (the discount rate).</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> PV = FV / (1 + r)<sup>n</sup> = FV x [1 / (1 + r)<sup>n</sup>]</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - present value</b><br>What is the present value of Rs 50,000 to be received after 3 years if the discount rate is 10%?<br>PV = 50,000 / (1.10)<sup>3</sup> = 50,000 / 1.331 = <b>Rs 37,565.74</b>.</div><h4>Extra (not in the syllabus list, but in your notes): compound growth rate and doubling period</h4><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Compound growth rate = (Ending value / Beginning value)<sup>1/n</sup> - 1 &nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp; Doubling period (Rule of 72) = 72 / interest rate (%)</div><p>At 9% a sum doubles in about 72 / 9 = <b>8 years</b> (exact calculation: 8.04 years).</p><h2>1.5 Relationship between present value and the discount rate</h2><p>Present value and the discount rate have an <b>inverse relationship</b>: as the discount rate rises, the present value of a given future sum falls, and vice versa. A higher discount rate means a higher opportunity cost (or higher risk), so less has to be set aside today to reach the same future amount.</p><table><tr><th>Future sum (Rs)</th><th>Years</th><th>Discount rate</th><th>Present value (Rs)</th></tr><tr><td>1,000</td><td>5</td><td>5%</td><td>783.53</td></tr><tr><td>1,000</td><td>5</td><td>10%</td><td>620.92</td></tr><tr><td>1,000</td><td>5</td><td>15%</td><td>497.18</td></tr></table><ul><li><b>Present value and time (n):</b> also inverse - the further away a cash flow, the lower its present value at a given discount rate.</li><li><b>Future value and rate:</b> direct - a higher rate gives a higher future value.</li></ul><div class='box'><b>Remember:</b> PV falls as the discount rate rises; FV rises as the rate rises.</div><h2>1.6 Present value and future value of an annuity</h2><p>An <b>annuity</b> is a series of equal payments or receipts made at equal intervals for a fixed period - for example loan EMIs, insurance premiums, pensions.</p><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>When payments are made</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Ordinary annuity (annuity regular)</td><td>At the <b>end</b> of each period</td><td>Loan EMI paid at month-end</td></tr><tr><td>Annuity due</td><td>At the <b>beginning</b> of each period</td><td>Rent paid at the start of the month</td></tr></table><h4>Future value of an annuity (ordinary)</h4><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> FVA = A x [ ((1 + r)<sup>n</sup> - 1) / r ] &nbsp;&nbsp; (A = equal periodic payment)</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - future value of an annuity</b><br>Rs 5,000 is deposited at the end of every year for 5 years at 10%.<br>FVA = 5,000 x [(1.10<sup>5</sup> - 1) / 0.10] = 5,000 x 6.1051 = <b>Rs 30,525.50</b>.<br>If the deposits were made at the <b>beginning</b> of each year (annuity due): 30,525.50 x 1.10 = <b>Rs 33,578.05</b>.</div><h4>Present value of an annuity (ordinary)</h4><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> PVA = A x [ (1 - (1 + r)<sup>-n</sup>) / r ]</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - present value of an annuity</b><br>What is the present value of Rs 10,000 received at the end of each year for 4 years if the discount rate is 12%?<br>PVA = 10,000 x [(1 - 1.12<sup>-4</sup>) / 0.12] = 10,000 x 3.0373 = <b>Rs 30,373.49</b>.<br>For an annuity due: 30,373.49 x 1.12 = <b>Rs 34,018.31</b>.</div><ul><li><b>Annuity due = ordinary annuity x (1 + r)</b>, for both PV and FV.</li><li>Each payment in an annuity is discounted (or compounded) separately; the formula or table adds them in one step.</li><li>Earlier payments earn interest for longer, so they contribute more to the future value.</li></ul><h4>Extra: perpetuity</h4><p>A <b>perpetuity</b> is an annuity that continues for ever. <b>PV = A / r</b>. Rs 5,000 a year for ever at 10% is worth 5,000 / 0.10 = <b>Rs 50,000</b> today.</p><h2>1.7 Valuation of bonds</h2><p>A <b>bond (debenture)</b> is a long-term debt instrument that pays periodic interest (the <b>coupon</b>) and returns the <b>face (par) value</b> at maturity. <b>Bond valuation</b> finds its fair price as the <b>present value of all expected cash flows</b>, discounted at the required rate of return (market rate).</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Bond value V = I x PVIFA(r, n) + M x PVIF(r, n) &nbsp;&nbsp; = &nbsp; I x [ (1 - (1 + r)<sup>-n</sup>) / r ] + M / (1 + r)<sup>n</sup><br>I = annual interest (coupon rate x face value), M = maturity (face) value, r = required rate, n = years to maturity</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - bond valuation</b><br>A Rs 1,000 bond with a 10% coupon matures in 5 years. Find its value if the required rate of return is (a) 12%, (b) 8%, (c) 10%.<br>Annual interest I = Rs 100.<br>(a) r = 12%: V = 100 x 3.6048 + 1,000 x 0.5674 = <b>about Rs 927.90</b> (discount).<br>(b) r = 8%: V = 100 x 3.9927 + 1,000 x 0.6806 = <b>Rs 1,079.85</b> (premium).<br>(c) r = 10%: V = <b>Rs 1,000</b> (par).</div><table><tr><th>Coupon rate vs required (market) rate</th><th>Bond sells at</th></tr><tr><td>Coupon = market rate</td><td>Par (face value)</td></tr><tr><td>Coupon > market rate</td><td>Premium (above face value)</td></tr><tr><td>Coupon < market rate</td><td>Discount (below face value)</td></tr></table><ul><li><b>Bond price and interest rates move in opposite directions</b> - when market rates rise, existing fixed-coupon bonds fall in value.</li><li><b>Yield to maturity (YTM)</b> is the rate that makes the present value of the bond's cash flows equal to its market price.</li><li>A <b>zero-coupon bond</b> pays no interest; its value is only M / (1 + r)<sup>n</sup>.</li><li>Stock valuation is <i>not</i> in the syllabus list; only bonds are required.</li></ul><div class='nextsteps'><span>Next steps - Unit 1</span><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/qa'>✍️ Detailed written answers</a><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/quiz'>▶ Practise MCQs</a><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/formulas'>🧮 Formula sheet</a></div>"}, {"id": "u2-new", "title": "Unit II-B · Complete notes - capital budgeting techniques", "html": "<div class='box'><b>UNIT II - Capital Budgeting (7 lectures).</b> Complete notes with formulas and worked examples, written for the new syllabus (Syllabus image FINANCE/syllabus.jpeg) from your Complete Notes file, unit slides and Unit 1-3 notes.</div><h2>2.1 Meaning and characteristics of capital budgeting</h2><p><b>Capital budgeting</b> is the process of planning and evaluating <b>long-term investment decisions</b> - whether to buy machinery, build a factory, launch a product or expand - where large sums are committed for a long time and the decision is hard to reverse. It is the practical form of the <b>investment decision</b>.</p><h4>Characteristics</h4><ul><li>Long-term effect on the firm.</li><li>Large funds involved.</li><li>Decisions are difficult and costly to reverse.</li><li>Involve risk and uncertainty about future cash flows.</li><li>Affect future profitability and growth.</li></ul><table><tr><th>Capital expenditure</th><th>Revenue expenditure</th></tr><tr><td>Benefit for a long period</td><td>Benefit for a short period</td></tr><tr><td>Purchase of assets (machine, building)</td><td>Day-to-day expenses (electricity bill, wages)</td></tr><tr><td>Large amount, done occasionally</td><td>Small amount, done frequently</td></tr></table><h2>2.2 Objectives and importance</h2><h4>Objectives</h4><ul><li><b>Select the most profitable projects</b> and maximise shareholder wealth.</li><li><b>Allocate scarce capital efficiently</b> among competing proposals.</li><li><b>Minimise investment risk</b> by systematic evaluation.</li><li><b>Support long-term growth and survival.</b></li><li><b>Control capital expenditure</b> - compare actual results with plans.</li></ul><h4>Kinds of investment proposals</h4><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Replacement</td><td>Replacing worn-out or obsolete equipment</td></tr><tr><td>Expansion</td><td>Increasing capacity of existing products</td></tr><tr><td>Diversification</td><td>Entering new products or markets</td></tr><tr><td>Research and development</td><td>Investing in new products and technology</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory / welfare</td><td>Required by law or for safety and employee welfare</td></tr></table><h4>Factors affecting capital budgeting</h4><ul><li>Cost of investment, expected profits, risk, interest rate, government policy, inflation, market demand and technology.</li></ul><h4>Advantages and limitations</h4><table><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Limitations</th></tr><tr><td><ul><li>Better decisions and planning</li><li>Higher profitability</li><li>Efficient use of funds</li><li>Reduces risk</li><li>Maximises shareholder wealth</li></ul></td><td><ul><li>Future is uncertain</li><li>Cash flow estimates may be wrong</li><li>Time-consuming</li><li>Needs technical knowledge and data</li></ul></td></tr></table><h2>2.3 Process of capital budgeting</h2><div class='box'><b>Flow:</b> Identify investment proposals &rarr; Screen and evaluate &rarr; Estimate cash flows &rarr; Select the project &rarr; Implement &rarr; Performance review (post-audit)</div><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th></tr><tr><td>1. Identification</td><td>Proposals arise from production, marketing or top management</td></tr><tr><td>2. Screening and evaluation</td><td>Technical feasibility, cost, expected benefits and risk are examined with capital budgeting techniques</td></tr><tr><td>3. Estimating cash flows</td><td>Initial outlay and yearly cash inflows over the project's life - the most critical step</td></tr><tr><td>4. Selection</td><td>Choose using payback, NPV, PI or IRR and the funds available</td></tr><tr><td>5. Implementation</td><td>Funds are released and the project is carried out</td></tr><tr><td>6. Performance review</td><td>Actual results are compared with forecasts to learn for the future</td></tr></table><h4>Cash flows - a note</h4><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Cash flow after tax (CFAT) = Profit after tax (PAT) + Depreciation</div><p>Capital budgeting uses <b>cash flows, not accounting profit</b>. Depreciation is added back because it is a non-cash expense. Initial investment includes the cost of the asset plus installation and working capital.</p><h2>2.4 Payback period method</h2><p><b>Payback period</b> is the time a project takes to recover its initial investment from its cash inflows. It answers: <i>how quickly do I get my money back?</i></p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Equal inflows: Payback period = Initial investment / Annual cash inflow<br>Unequal inflows: add the inflows year by year until the investment is recovered; interpolate within the final year.</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - equal inflows</b><br>A project costs Rs 5,00,000 and gives Rs 1,25,000 a year.<br>Payback = 5,00,000 / 1,25,000 = <b>4 years</b>.</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - unequal inflows</b><br>Investment Rs 4,00,000. Inflows: Year 1 Rs 1,00,000; Year 2 Rs 1,50,000; Year 3 Rs 2,00,000; Year 4 Rs 2,50,000.<br>Cumulative after 2 years = 2,50,000; balance to recover = 1,50,000.<br>Payback = 2 + (1,50,000 / 2,00,000) = <b>2.75 years</b>.</div><p><b>Decision rule:</b> accept if the payback period is less than or equal to the target; between projects choose the shorter payback.</p><table><tr><th>Merits</th><th>Limitations</th></tr><tr><td><ul><li>Simple and easy to calculate</li><li>Useful where liquidity or risk is a concern</li><li>Rough measure of risk - a shorter payback means less risk</li></ul></td><td><ul><li><b>Ignores time value of money</b></li><li><b>Ignores cash flows after the payback period</b></li><li>Does not measure profitability</li></ul></td></tr></table><p><b>Discounted payback</b> removes the first limitation by discounting the cash flows before adding them.</p><h2>2.5 Accounting rate of return (ARR) - extra</h2><p>ARR (in your notes, though not named in the syllabus topic list) measures the average annual accounting profit as a percentage of investment.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> ARR = (Average annual profit after tax / Investment) x 100 &nbsp;&nbsp; (investment = original investment or average investment = (cost + scrap) / 2)</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - ARR</b><br>Investment Rs 1,00,000 (no scrap), average annual profit after tax Rs 15,000.<br>ARR on original investment = 15,000 / 1,00,000 = <b>15%</b>.<br>ARR on average investment = 15,000 / 50,000 = <b>30%</b>.</div><p>Merit: simple, uses profit data. Limitation: uses accounting profit, ignores the time value of money.</p><h2>2.6 Net present value (NPV) method</h2><p><b>NPV</b> is the difference between the present value of the project's cash inflows and its initial investment, discounted at the firm's required rate of return (cost of capital). It shows directly the <b>addition to shareholder wealth</b>.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> NPV = [ CF<sub>1</sub>/(1+k) + CF<sub>2</sub>/(1+k)<sup>2</sup> + ... + CF<sub>n</sub>/(1+k)<sup>n</sup> ] - Initial investment</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - NPV</b><br>Initial investment Rs 1,00,000. Cash inflows: Year 1 Rs 40,000; Year 2 Rs 50,000; Year 3 Rs 60,000. Cost of capital 10%.<br>PV of inflows = 40,000 / 1.10 + 50,000 / 1.21 + 60,000 / 1.331 = 36,363.64 + 41,322.31 + 45,078.89 = Rs 1,22,764.84.<br>NPV = 1,22,764.84 - 1,00,000 = <b>Rs 22,764.84</b> &rarr; positive, so <b>accept</b>.</div><table><tr><th>NPV</th><th>Decision</th></tr><tr><td>NPV > 0</td><td>Accept - adds to wealth</td></tr><tr><td>NPV < 0</td><td>Reject - reduces wealth</td></tr><tr><td>NPV = 0</td><td>Project earns exactly the required return; decide on other factors</td></tr></table><table><tr><th>Merits</th><th>Limitations</th></tr><tr><td><ul><li>Considers time value of money</li><li>Uses all cash flows of the whole life</li><li>Directly linked to wealth maximisation</li><li>Most theoretically sound method</li></ul></td><td><ul><li>Needs an accurate discount rate</li><li>Absolute figure - hard to compare projects of different size</li><li>More complex than payback</li></ul></td></tr></table><h2>2.7 Profitability index (PI)</h2><p>The <b>profitability index</b> (benefit-cost ratio) is the present value of cash inflows per rupee of investment - a relative form of NPV.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> PI = Present value of cash inflows / Initial investment</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - PI</b><br>From the example above: PI = 1,22,764.84 / 1,00,000 = <b>1.228</b> &rarr; greater than 1, accept.</div><table><tr><th>PI</th><th>Decision</th></tr><tr><td>PI > 1</td><td>Accept (equivalent to NPV > 0)</td></tr><tr><td>PI < 1</td><td>Reject</td></tr><tr><td>PI = 1</td><td>Indifferent (NPV = 0)</td></tr></table><p>PI is useful for ranking projects of <b>different sizes</b> and under <b>capital rationing</b> (limited funds). It can occasionally rank projects differently from NPV when sizes differ greatly.</p><h2>2.8 Internal rate of return (IRR)</h2><p><b>IRR</b> is the discount rate at which the present value of inflows equals the present value of outflows - the rate at which <b>NPV = 0</b>. It is the project's own expected rate of return.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Interpolation: IRR = Lower rate + [ NPV at lower rate / (NPV at lower rate - NPV at higher rate) ] x Difference between the rates</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - IRR</b><br>Same project (investment Rs 1,00,000; inflows 40,000, 50,000, 60,000).<br>At 20%: NPV = + Rs 2,777.78. At 22%: NPV = - Rs 577.58.<br>IRR = 20 + [2,777.78 / (2,777.78 + 577.58)] x 2 = 20 + 1.66 = <b>about 21.66%</b> (exact 21.65%).<br>Cost of capital is 10%, so IRR is higher &rarr; <b>accept</b>.</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - IRR for equal inflows</b><br>Investment Rs 1,00,000; inflow Rs 30,000 a year for 5 years.<br>Factor = 1,00,000 / 30,000 = 3.333. On the annuity table (5 years) 3.352 is at 15% and 3.274 at 16%, so IRR is a little above 15% (about <b>15.2%</b>).</div><p><b>Decision rule:</b> accept if IRR is greater than the cost of capital; reject if lower.</p><table><tr><th>Merits</th><th>Limitations</th></tr><tr><td><ul><li>Considers time value of money</li><li>Gives a percentage that is easy to compare</li><li>Does not need the discount rate in advance</li></ul></td><td><ul><li>Trial and error / interpolation needed</li><li>Can give multiple or misleading IRRs for non-conventional cash flows</li><li>May conflict with NPV for mutually exclusive projects</li></ul></td></tr></table><h2>2.9 Comparison of the techniques</h2><table><tr><th>Basis</th><th>Payback</th><th>NPV</th><th>PI</th><th>IRR</th></tr><tr><td>Time value of money</td><td>Ignored</td><td>Considered</td><td>Considered</td><td>Considered</td></tr><tr><td>Measures</td><td>Time to recover investment</td><td>Absolute wealth added</td><td>Relative profitability</td><td>Rate of return</td></tr><tr><td>Decision rule</td><td>Accept if within target period</td><td>Accept if NPV > 0</td><td>Accept if PI > 1</td><td>Accept if IRR > cost of capital</td></tr><tr><td>Best suited for</td><td>Quick liquidity / risk screening</td><td>Overall wealth maximisation</td><td>Projects of different size, capital rationing</td><td>Comparing returns across options</td></tr><tr><td>Main limitation</td><td>Ignores cash flows after payback</td><td>Needs an accurate discount rate</td><td>May conflict with NPV on size</td><td>Multiple IRRs, may conflict with NPV</td></tr></table><h4>NPV and IRR compared</h4><table><tr><th>Similarities</th><th>Differences</th></tr><tr><td><ul><li>Both use discounted cash flows</li><li>Both consider the whole life of the project</li><li>Both give the same accept / reject answer for a single conventional project</li></ul></td><td><ul><li>NPV gives a rupee value, IRR a percentage.</li><li>NPV assumes cash inflows are reinvested at the cost of capital; IRR assumes they are reinvested at the IRR.</li><li>NPV needs the discount rate beforehand; IRR does not.</li><li>For mutually exclusive projects of different scale or timing they can conflict - <b>NPV is preferred</b> because it measures wealth creation directly.</li></ul></td></tr></table><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - choosing between two projects</b><br>Project A: investment Rs 1,00,000, NPV at 10% = Rs 22,764.84, PI = 1.228. Project B: investment Rs 3,00,000, inflows 1,00,000, 1,20,000, 1,40,000, 1,60,000, cost of capital 12%: NPV = Rs 86,281.11, PI = 1.288.<br>B has the higher NPV (adds more wealth) and the higher PI, so B is preferred.</div><div class='nextsteps'><span>Next steps - Unit 2</span><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/qa'>✍️ Detailed written answers</a><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/quiz'>▶ Practise MCQs</a><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/formulas'>🧮 Formula sheet</a></div>"}, {"id": "u3-new", "title": "Unit III-B · Complete notes - capital structure, leverage, cost of capital", "html": "<div class='box'><b>UNIT III - Capital Structure (7 lectures).</b> Complete notes with formulas and worked examples, written for the new syllabus (Syllabus image FINANCE/syllabus.jpeg) from your Complete Notes file, unit slides and Unit 1-3 notes.</div><h2>3.1 Concept of capital structure</h2><p><b>Capital structure</b> is the mix or proportion of the long-term sources of funds - debt, preference capital, equity and retained earnings - used to finance the firm. It is the result of the <b>financing decision</b> and answers: in what proportion should funds be raised from debt and from equity?</p><table><tr><th>Component</th><th>Nature</th></tr><tr><td>Equity share capital</td><td>Owners' funds; no fixed dividend; carries the highest risk and highest cost</td></tr><tr><td>Preference share capital</td><td>Hybrid; fixed dividend, senior to equity but junior to debt; dividend not tax-deductible</td></tr><tr><td>Debt (debentures, long-term loans)</td><td>Borrowed funds; fixed interest whether or not profit is earned; interest is tax-deductible</td></tr><tr><td>Retained earnings</td><td>Profit ploughed back; cheap internal source</td></tr></table><p><b>Optimum capital structure</b> is the mix of debt and equity that <b>minimises the overall cost of capital and maximises the value of the firm</b> (and so shareholder wealth).</p><h4>Features of an appropriate capital structure</h4><ul><li><b>Profitability</b> - maximum return at minimum cost.</li><li><b>Solvency</b> - not so much debt that the firm cannot meet interest.</li><li><b>Flexibility</b> - room to raise more funds later.</li><li><b>Control</b> - does not dilute owners' control unduly.</li><li><b>Simplicity</b> - easy to understand and manage.</li></ul><h2>3.2 Factors determining capital structure</h2><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Explanation</th></tr><tr><td>Cost of capital</td><td>Debt is cheaper (tax shield), so some debt lowers the overall cost - but only up to a point</td></tr><tr><td>Financial risk</td><td>More debt means fixed interest obligations and greater risk of insolvency, especially if earnings are unstable</td></tr><tr><td>Control</td><td>New equity dilutes control; debt does not - owners wanting control prefer debt</td></tr><tr><td>Flexibility</td><td>Keeping unused borrowing capacity for the future</td></tr><tr><td>Cash flow and debt-servicing capacity</td><td>Expected cash flows must comfortably cover interest and principal</td></tr><tr><td>Nature of industry / business risk</td><td>Stable-demand industries (utilities) can carry more debt than cyclical ones</td></tr><tr><td>Legal and regulatory requirements</td><td>Lender covenants, debt-equity limits, company law</td></tr><tr><td>Market conditions</td><td>A strong share market favours equity issues; low interest rates favour debt</td></tr><tr><td>Size and stage of the firm; tax policy</td><td>Large, established firms have easier access to debt; tax-deductible interest encourages debt</td></tr></table><h4>Extra: capital structure theories (in your Unit 3 notes, beyond the syllabus list)</h4><table><tr><th>Theory</th><th>Idea</th></tr><tr><td>Net Income (NI) approach</td><td>Cost of debt is lower than cost of equity and both stay constant; more debt lowers WACC and raises firm value, so value is maximised with maximum debt</td></tr><tr><td>Net Operating Income (NOI) approach</td><td>WACC is constant; more debt raises the cost of equity by exactly the saving, so capital structure does not affect value</td></tr><tr><td>Modigliani-Miller (MM)</td><td>Extension of NOI; without taxes firm value is independent of capital structure (proved by the arbitrage process); with taxes value rises with debt</td></tr></table><h2>3.3 Leverage - meaning, objectives and uses</h2><p><b>Leverage</b> is the use of fixed-cost items - fixed operating costs or fixed financial costs (interest, preference dividend) - to <b>magnify the effect of a change in sales on profit</b>. Like a lever, a small push on one end moves a large load, and it works both ways: gains and losses are both magnified.</p><h4>Objectives and uses</h4><ul><li>Magnify the returns to equity shareholders when the return on investment exceeds the cost of fixed-cost funds.</li><li>Measure risk - how sensitive profit is to changes in sales.</li><li>Plan the capital structure - decide how much debt to use.</li><li>Help investors and managers judge the riskiness of earnings.</li></ul><h2>3.4 Operating, financial and combined leverage</h2><h4>Operating leverage</h4><p>Arises from <b>fixed operating costs</b> (rent, depreciation, salaries). It measures how sensitive <b>EBIT</b> is to a change in <b>sales</b>. High fixed costs give high operating leverage and <b>high business risk</b>.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Degree of operating leverage (DOL) = Contribution / EBIT = % change in EBIT / % change in sales<br>Contribution = Sales - Variable cost; EBIT = Contribution - Fixed operating cost</div><h4>Financial leverage</h4><p>Arises from <b>fixed financial costs</b> (interest on debt, preference dividend). It measures how sensitive <b>EPS</b> is to a change in <b>EBIT</b>. More debt gives <b>high financial risk</b>. When the return on investment is above the cost of debt, leverage helps equity holders - this is <b>trading on equity</b>; when below, it hurts.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Degree of financial leverage (DFL) = EBIT / EBT = EBIT / (EBIT - Interest) = % change in EPS / % change in EBIT<br>(with preference dividend: DFL = EBIT / [EBIT - Interest - Preference dividend / (1 - t)])</div><h4>Combined (total) leverage</h4><p>The <b>combined effect</b> of operating and financial leverage: it measures how sensitive <b>EPS</b> is to a change in <b>sales</b> and shows <b>total risk</b>.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Degree of combined leverage (DCL) = DOL x DFL = Contribution / EBT = % change in EPS / % change in sales</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - all three leverages</b><br>Sales Rs 1,00,000; variable cost Rs 40,000; fixed operating cost Rs 20,000; interest Rs 10,000.<br>Contribution = 1,00,000 - 40,000 = Rs 60,000. EBIT = 60,000 - 20,000 = Rs 40,000. EBT = 40,000 - 10,000 = Rs 30,000.<br>DOL = 60,000 / 40,000 = <b>1.5</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; DFL = 40,000 / 30,000 = <b>1.33</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; DCL = 1.5 x 1.33 = 60,000 / 30,000 = <b>2.0</b>.<br>Check: if sales rise 10% to Rs 1,10,000, variable cost = 44,000, contribution = 66,000, EBIT = 46,000 (up 15% = 1.5 x 10%), EBT = 36,000 (up 20% = 2.0 x 10%). A 10% fall in sales would cut EPS by 20%.</div><table><tr><th>Basis</th><th>Operating leverage</th><th>Financial leverage</th><th>Combined leverage</th></tr><tr><td>Arises from</td><td>Fixed operating costs</td><td>Fixed financial costs (interest, preference dividend)</td><td>Both</td></tr><tr><td>Measures sensitivity of</td><td>EBIT to sales</td><td>EPS to EBIT</td><td>EPS to sales</td></tr><tr><td>Risk indicated</td><td>Business risk</td><td>Financial risk</td><td>Total risk</td></tr><tr><td>Formula</td><td>Contribution / EBIT</td><td>EBIT / EBT</td><td>Contribution / EBT</td></tr></table><p><b>Extra - EBIT-EPS analysis:</b> compares financing plans (debt vs equity) at different EBIT levels; the <b>indifference point</b> is the EBIT at which EPS is equal under both plans. Above it, debt gives higher EPS.</p><h2>3.5 Cost of capital - concept and importance</h2><p><b>Cost of capital</b> is the minimum rate of return a firm must earn on its investments to satisfy the expectations of its suppliers of capital (lenders, preference holders and equity holders). It is the 'price' of funds and the <b>discount rate</b> used in NPV.</p><ul><li><b>Capital budgeting:</b> accept a project only if its return is more than the cost of capital.</li><li><b>Capital structure:</b> helps find the mix with the lowest overall cost.</li><li><b>Performance benchmark:</b> shows whether the firm creates or destroys value.</li><li><b>Dividend and working capital decisions</b> also use it.</li></ul><p>Order of cost: <b>debt &lt; preference &lt; equity</b>, because of the tax shield on interest and the lower risk of lenders.</p><h2>3.6 Cost of debt, preference shares and equity shares</h2><h4>Cost of debt (Kd)</h4><p>Interest is a tax-deductible expense, so the <b>after-tax cost of debt is lower</b> than the interest rate.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Irredeemable debt: Kd = I (1 - t) / NP &nbsp;&nbsp; | &nbsp;&nbsp; Redeemable debt: Kd = [ I (1 - t) + (RV - NP)/n ] / [ (RV + NP)/2 ]<br>I = interest, t = tax rate, NP = net proceeds, RV = redemption value, n = years</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - cost of debt</b><br>10% debentures of Rs 100 face value are issued at Rs 95 (net proceeds), redeemable at par after 10 years; tax rate 30%.<br>Kd = [10 (0.70) + (100 - 95)/10] / [(100 + 95)/2] = (7 + 0.5) / 97.5 = <b>7.69%</b>.<br>Simple case: 12% debt, tax 30%, issued at par: Kd = 12 x (1 - 0.30) = <b>8.4%</b>.</div><h4>Cost of preference shares (Kp)</h4><p>Preference dividend is paid out of after-tax profit, so there is <b>no tax saving</b>.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Irredeemable: Kp = D / NP &nbsp;&nbsp; | &nbsp;&nbsp; Redeemable: Kp = [ D + (RV - NP)/n ] / [ (RV + NP)/2 ]</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - cost of preference shares</b><br>10% preference shares (Rs 100) issued at Rs 95, redeemable at par after 10 years: Kp = (10 + 0.5) / 97.5 = <b>10.77%</b>.</div><h4>Cost of equity (Ke)</h4><p>Equity is the <b>costliest</b> source because equity holders bear the most risk and dividends bring no tax saving.</p><table><tr><th>Method</th><th>Formula</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Dividend yield + growth (Gordon)</td><td>Ke = D<sub>1</sub> / P<sub>0</sub> + g</td><td>D<sub>1</sub> = Rs 4, P<sub>0</sub> = Rs 50, g = 5%: Ke = 8% + 5% = <b>13%</b></td></tr><tr><td>Earnings yield</td><td>Ke = E / P</td><td>EPS Rs 6, price Rs 50: Ke = <b>12%</b></td></tr><tr><td>CAPM</td><td>Ke = Rf + Beta x (Rm - Rf)</td><td>Rf = 7%, Rm = 12%, beta = 1.2: Ke = 7 + 1.2 x 5 = <b>13%</b></td></tr></table><p><b>Cost of retained earnings</b> is taken equal to the cost of equity (shareholders could have earned that return if the profit had been paid out).</p><h2>3.7 Weighted average cost of capital (WACC)</h2><p><b>WACC</b> is the overall cost of capital - the average of the costs of all sources, weighted by their proportion in the total capital structure.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> WACC = (Wd x Kd) + (Wp x Kp) + (We x Ke) &nbsp;&nbsp; = &nbsp; Sum of (Amount x Cost) / Total capital</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - WACC</b><br>Capital: Debt Rs 5,00,000 (before-tax cost 10%, tax rate 30% so after-tax 7%); Preference Rs 2,00,000 (12%); Equity Rs 3,00,000 (16%). Total Rs 10,00,000.<br>WACC = (5,00,000 x 7% + 2,00,000 x 12% + 3,00,000 x 16%) / 10,00,000 = (35,000 + 24,000 + 48,000) / 10,00,000 = <b>10.7%</b>.</div><ul><li><b>Weights:</b> use book value or market value (market value is more accurate; target weights are used for planning).</li><li><b>Importance:</b> discount rate for capital budgeting; benchmark for performance; guide to the optimal capital structure, which is the mix with the lowest WACC.</li><li><b>Factors affecting WACC:</b> interest rates, tax rate, capital structure mix, the firm's risk, market conditions and dividend policy.</li></ul><div class='nextsteps'><span>Next steps - Unit 3</span><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/qa'>✍️ Detailed written answers</a><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/quiz'>▶ Practise MCQs</a><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/formulas'>🧮 Formula sheet</a></div>"}, {"id": "u4-new", "title": "Unit IV · Complete notes - dividend models, working capital", "html": "<div class='box'><b>UNIT IV - Dividend Decision and Working Capital (6 lectures).</b> Complete notes with formulas and worked examples, written for the new syllabus (Syllabus image FINANCE/syllabus.jpeg) from your Complete Notes file, unit slides and Unit 1-3 notes.</div><h2>4.1 Concept of dividend decision</h2><p><b>Dividend</b> is the part of after-tax profit distributed to shareholders in proportion to their shareholding. The <b>dividend decision</b> is the third major decision of financial management: how much of the earnings to <b>pay out</b> and how much to <b>retain</b> for reinvestment. It is closely linked to financing, because retained earnings are one of the cheapest internal sources of funds.</p><h4>Objectives of the dividend decision</h4><ul><li>Maximise shareholder wealth (dividends plus capital gains).</li><li>Provide funds for growth through retained earnings.</li><li>Maintain a stable and fair record that investors can trust.</li><li>Keep enough liquidity and flexibility.</li></ul><h4>Forms of dividend</h4><ul><li><b>Cash dividend</b> (most common), <b>stock dividend</b> (bonus shares), <b>interim</b> and <b>final</b> dividend, and share buy-back as an alternative way to return cash.</li></ul><h2>4.2 Significance of dividend policy</h2><p><b>Dividend policy</b> is the set of principles a company follows to decide, consistently over time, how much to pay as dividend and how much to retain.</p><table><tr><th>Significance</th><th>Explanation</th></tr><tr><td>Signal of performance</td><td>Steady or rising dividends show financial health and management's confidence</td></tr><tr><td>Shareholder wealth</td><td>Decides how wealth is received - dividends now or capital gains later</td></tr><tr><td>Growth and capital structure</td><td>Higher retention gives more cheap internal funds and less need for outside finance</td></tr><tr><td>Investor clientele</td><td>Retirees like regular income; growth investors prefer retention</td></tr><tr><td>Share price</td><td>Dividend changes carry information and move the market price</td></tr><tr><td>Financial flexibility and market reputation</td><td>A consistent policy builds credibility and eases fund raising</td></tr></table><h2>4.3 Factors affecting the dividend decision</h2><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Explanation</th></tr><tr><td>Profitability and stability of earnings</td><td>Steady profits support steady dividends</td></tr><tr><td>Liquidity position</td><td>Profit is not cash; dividends need actual cash</td></tr><tr><td>Growth and investment opportunities</td><td>Many profitable projects mean more retention</td></tr><tr><td>Legal and contractual restrictions</td><td>Company law and loan covenants limit payout</td></tr><tr><td>Tax considerations</td><td>Tax on dividends vs capital gains</td></tr><tr><td>Shareholder expectations</td><td>A sudden cut is read as bad news</td></tr><tr><td>Access to external finance</td><td>Easy access to funds allows higher payout</td></tr><tr><td>Inflation</td><td>Firms retain more to replace assets at higher prices</td></tr><tr><td>Control, existing debt and the economy</td><td>Fear of dilution, debt repayment burden, general business conditions</td></tr></table><h4>Types of dividend policy</h4><table><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Stable dividend policy</td><td>A fixed amount or steadily growing dividend each year</td></tr><tr><td>Constant payout ratio</td><td>A fixed percentage of profit is paid, so dividend moves with profit</td></tr><tr><td>Regular plus extra dividend</td><td>A low regular dividend plus an extra payment in good years</td></tr><tr><td>Residual dividend policy</td><td>Pay dividends only from profit left after financing all profitable investments</td></tr></table><h2>4.4 Relevance dividend models - Walter and Gordon</h2><p>There are two schools of thought. The <b>relevance school</b> says dividend policy changes the value of the firm (Walter, Gordon). The <b>irrelevance school</b> (Modigliani-Miller) says that under perfect markets it does not. The syllabus covers the two relevance models.</p><h4>Walter's model</h4><p>James Walter: the dividend policy almost always affects the value of the firm, depending on the relation between the firm's <b>internal rate of return (r)</b> and its <b>cost of capital (k)</b>.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> P = [ D + (r / k)(E - D) ] / k &nbsp;&nbsp; P = market price per share, D = dividend per share, E = earnings per share, r = return on investment, k = cost of capital</div><table><tr><th>Type of firm</th><th>Condition</th><th>Best payout</th></tr><tr><td>Growth firm</td><td>r > k</td><td>Retain all earnings (zero payout) - value rises as dividend falls</td></tr><tr><td>Normal firm</td><td>r = k</td><td>Any payout - value is the same</td></tr><tr><td>Declining firm</td><td>r < k</td><td>Pay out all earnings (100% payout)</td></tr></table><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - Walter's model</b><br>E = Rs 8 per share, k = 10%, r = 15% (growth firm).<br>D = 0: P = [0 + (0.15/0.10)(8)] / 0.10 = <b>Rs 120</b>.   D = 2: P = [2 + 1.5 x 6] / 0.10 = <b>Rs 110</b>.   D = 4: <b>Rs 100</b>.   D = 6: <b>Rs 90</b>.<br>Price falls as the payout rises, so retain. If r were 8% (declining firm): D = 0 gives Rs 64, D = 6 gives Rs 76 - price rises with payout, so pay out.</div><b>Assumptions:</b> all financing by retained earnings (no outside debt or equity); r and k constant; infinite life; no change in EPS and DPS once decided.<h4>Gordon's model (dividend growth model)</h4><p>Myron Gordon also holds that dividend policy affects value, using the <b>'bird-in-hand' argument</b>: investors are risk-averse and prefer a certain dividend today to an uncertain capital gain tomorrow, so they discount future gains at a higher rate. The firm that pays a higher dividend commands a higher price.</p><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> P<sub>0</sub> = E (1 - b) / (k - b r) = D<sub>1</sub> / (k - g) &nbsp;&nbsp; b = retention ratio, r = return on investment, k = cost of equity, g = growth rate = b x r (k must be greater than g)</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - Gordon's model</b><br>E = Rs 10 per share, k = 12%, r = 15%, retention ratio b = 50%.<br>g = 0.50 x 0.15 = 7.5%.  P<sub>0</sub> = 10 (1 - 0.50) / (0.12 - 0.075) = 5 / 0.045 = <b>Rs 111.11</b>.<br>With no retention (b = 0): P<sub>0</sub> = 10 / 0.12 = Rs 83.33.</div><b>Assumptions:</b> all-equity firm; no outside financing; r and k constant; retention ratio constant; k greater than g.<table><tr><th>Basis</th><th>Walter's model</th><th>Gordon's model</th></tr><tr><td>Core argument</td><td>Relevance depends on r versus k</td><td>Bird in hand - investors prefer certain dividends to uncertain gains</td></tr><tr><td>Key variable</td><td>Relation between r and k</td><td>Investor risk aversion over the timing and certainty of returns</td></tr><tr><td>Dividend recommended</td><td>High payout only when r < k</td><td>Generally a higher payout, because certainty is valued</td></tr><tr><td>Common ground</td><td>Both say dividend policy is relevant to firm value (against MM irrelevance)</td><td>Same</td></tr></table><p><b>Modigliani-Miller (irrelevance)</b>: in a perfect market value depends only on the firm's earning power and investment policy; shareholders can create their own 'dividend' by selling shares (home-made dividends).</p><h2>4.5 Working capital management - meaning and types</h2><p><b>Working capital</b> is the capital needed to finance the firm's <b>day-to-day operations</b> - it is invested in current assets such as cash, inventory and receivables. <b>Working capital management</b> means managing current assets and current liabilities so that the firm has enough liquidity without locking up excess funds.</p><table><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Gross working capital</td><td>Total investment in current assets</td></tr><tr><td>Net working capital</td><td>Current assets - current liabilities; positive NWC means the firm can meet short-term dues</td></tr><tr><td>Positive working capital</td><td>Current assets exceed current liabilities</td></tr><tr><td>Negative working capital</td><td>Current liabilities exceed current assets - liquidity danger</td></tr></table><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Permanent (fixed) working capital</td><td>Minimum level of current assets always needed, whatever the season; split into regular and reserve working capital</td></tr><tr><td>Temporary (variable) working capital</td><td>Extra working capital for seasonal or special needs (festival stock, order surge)</td></tr></table><h4>Objectives and importance</h4><ul><li>Maintain <b>liquidity</b> and pay dues on time; ensure <b>smooth operations</b>.</li><li>Improve <b>profitability</b> by avoiding idle cash and excess stock.</li><li>Avoid <b>over-investment</b> (idle funds) and <b>under-investment</b> (stock-outs, loss of sales).</li><li>Reduce <b>financial risk</b>, keep good <b>credit standing</b>, and support growth.</li></ul><h2>4.6 Operating cycle</h2><p>The <b>operating cycle</b> (working capital cycle) is the time between spending cash on raw materials and receiving cash from customers. A shorter cycle means faster recovery of funds and less working capital.</p><div class='box'><b>Flow:</b> Purchase raw materials &rarr; Store raw materials &rarr; Production (work in progress) &rarr; Store finished goods &rarr; Sale (debtors) &rarr; Collection of cash</div><div class='box'><b>Formula:</b> Operating cycle = R + W + F + D (gross operating cycle); Cash cycle = R + W + F + D - C<br>R = raw material storage period, W = work-in-progress period, F = finished goods storage period, D = debtors collection period, C = creditors payment period (all in days)</div><div class='eg'><b>Solved example - operating cycle</b><br>Raw material storage 30 days, work-in-progress 15 days, finished goods 10 days, debtors 40 days, creditors 35 days.<br>Gross operating cycle = 30 + 15 + 10 + 40 = <b>95 days</b>.<br>Net operating (cash) cycle = 95 - 35 = <b>60 days</b>.<br>If the firm's daily cost of operations is Rs 5,000, funds tied up for the 60-day cycle are about Rs 3,00,000.</div><p><b>Period formulae:</b> raw material days = average raw material stock / average daily consumption (e.g. Rs 60,000 / (Rs 7,20,000 / 360) = 30 days).</p><h2>4.7 Determinants of working capital requirement</h2><table><tr><th>Determinant</th><th>Effect on working capital</th></tr><tr><td>Nature of business</td><td>Manufacturing needs more than trading or service firms</td></tr><tr><td>Scale of operations</td><td>Larger sales and production need more</td></tr><tr><td>Length of operating cycle</td><td>A longer cycle ties up more funds</td></tr><tr><td>Credit policy</td><td>Liberal credit to customers raises receivables; credit from suppliers lowers need</td></tr><tr><td>Seasonality</td><td>Seasonal peaks need extra temporary working capital</td></tr><tr><td>Growth and expansion plans</td><td>Growing firms need more stock and receivables</td></tr><tr><td>Availability of raw materials</td><td>Irregular supply forces larger stocks</td></tr><tr><td>Business cycle</td><td>Boom needs more working capital; recession needs less</td></tr><tr><td>Dividend policy, profit margin, inflation and production policy</td><td>Retained profits add working capital; rising prices raise requirement</td></tr></table><p><b>Sources of working capital finance:</b> trade credit, bank overdraft and cash credit, short-term loans, commercial paper, factoring, and retained earnings for the permanent portion.</p><div class='nextsteps'><span>Next steps - Unit 4</span><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/qa'>✍️ Detailed written answers</a><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/quiz'>▶ Practise MCQs</a><a class='nbtn' href='#/finance/formulas'>🧮 Formula sheet</a></div>"}];
  pages.forEach((p) => { SUB.units.push(p); });
})();
